{
  "dom_id": "step-q42472",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 1 & 19 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 1 & 19 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-9.\n$$",
